### How do you group and filter employees with streams?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class EmployeeGrouping {
    record Employee(String name, String department, String city, int age, double salary) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
            new Employee("Rajesh", "IT", "Mumbai", 35, 85000),
            new Employee("Priya", "IT", "Delhi", 28, 72000),
            new Employee("Amit", "Finance", "Mumbai", 42, 95000),
            new Employee("Deepa", "Finance", "Chennai", 55, 110000),
            new Employee("Vikram", "IT", "Mumbai", 31, 78000)
        );

        // Group by city
        Map<String, List<Employee>> byCity = employees.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::city));
        System.out.println("Cities: " + byCity.keySet());

        // Count by department
        Map<String, Long> countByDept = employees.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::department, Collectors.counting()));
        System.out.println("Dept count: " + countByDept);
    }
}
```

```text
Cities: [Mumbai, Delhi, Chennai]
Dept count: {IT=3, Finance=2}
```

### How do you filter then group?

```java
// Filter Mumbai, then group by department
Map<String, List<Employee>> mumbaiByDept = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> "Mumbai".equals(e.city()))
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::department));
System.out.println("Mumbai depts: " + mumbaiByDept.keySet());
// {IT=[Rajesh, Vikram], Finance=[Amit]}
```

Filter first, then group. This avoids grouping all employees and then filtering each group.

### How do you filter by age?

```java
// Filter age > 40
List<Employee> seniors = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.age() > 40)
    .toList();
System.out.println("Seniors: " + seniors.stream().map(Employee::name).toList());
// [Amit, Deepa]
```

### How do you group by age decade?

```java
// Group by age decade
Map<Integer, List<Employee>> byDecade = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(e -> (e.age() / 10) * 10));
System.out.println("Decades: " + byDecade.keySet());
// [30, 20, 40, 50]
```

### How do you extract names per department?

```java
// Names per department (mapping downstream)
Map<String, List<String>> namesByDept = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::department,
        Collectors.mapping(Employee::name, Collectors.toList())));
System.out.println("IT names: " + namesByDept.get("IT"));
// [Rajesh, Priya, Vikram]
```

### How do you get sorted keys with TreeMap?

```java
// Sorted keys: TreeMap factory
Map<String, Long> sorted = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::city,
        TreeMap::new, Collectors.counting()));
System.out.println("Sorted: " + sorted);
// {Chennai=1, Delhi=1, Mumbai=3}
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: grouping all employees first, then filtering each group
Map<String, List<Employee>> all = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::city));
List<Employee> mumbai = all.get("Mumbai"); // two-step, inefficient

// CORRECT: filter first, then group
Map<String, List<Employee>> mumbaiByDept = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> "Mumbai".equals(e.city()))
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::department));
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Each grouping replaces a manual HashMap loop. The filter-then-group pattern avoids grouping all employees and then filtering each group.
- The mapping downstream produces lightweight results (names instead of full objects).
- TreeMap::new as the map factory produces alphabetically sorted keys.
- For multi-level grouping, nest groupingBy inside groupingBy.
- In production, push filter+group to database GROUP BY for large datasets.
